    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Gargano Promontory, located on the Adriatic coast of southern Italy, is a prominent highly elevated region which has been uplifted more than 1000 m above the adjacent Apulian foreland. Using detailed field mapping, combined with air photo interpretation, we have defined two main fault systems in the region which controlled the uplift of the Gargano Block. The first system is oriented NW-SE, shows primarily reverse to oblique-reverse displacements, and is pervasive throughout the Gargano. The second system is strikes E-W, bounds the northern and southern margins of the uplifted block, and extends east into the offshore Adriatic. The structural data collected indicate that the two systems have interacted to produce the uplift of the region. The geometry of the E-W fault system defines a right step in the left-lateral fault system, resulting in a zone of compression within the step. Uplift was accommodated by reverse motion on the NW-SE fault system as a result of increased compressional stress within the overstep region. This mechanism of uplift has not been recognized previously, and may also be found in deformed regions within the southern Apennines.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
